#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120688} :activity/id #uuid "01aaa671-aae7-587d-86f6-117d15d3fbee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049222 :topic/id #uuid "e4770b01-d5aa-5267-93ca-20f83709e3b1" :topic/math-academy-id 1855} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120688 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121040 :tutorial/content "A **basis** of a subspace $U$ (in $\\mathbb{R}^n$) is a set of vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that is linearly independent and spans $U.$\n\nFor example, consider the following subspace consisting of the span of three vectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find a basis of $U,$ we need to find a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $U.$\n\n**Watch out!** The set\n$[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nis *not* a basis of $U.$ It's true that this set *spans* $U,$ but the vectors in this set are *not* linearly independent. In particular, the vector $\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find a basis of $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ we can start with $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\},$ but we need to remove any \"redundant\" vectors that can be written as a linear combination of other vectors in the set. In this case, we remove ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}$ because it can be written as a linear combination of $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nWe are left with the set\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThese two vectors are linearly independent (since they are not parallel), and removing a redundant vector does not change the span, so these vectors still span $U.$ That is to say,\n\n$$\nU = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\} = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\mathcal{B} = \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ is a basis of $U.$\n\n**Note:** To see that the vectors $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ still span $U,$ first note that any vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in U$ can be written as a linear combination of the three original vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1} + x_2 {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} + x_3 {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} = -{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}+3{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2},$ then we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}(-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}-{x}_{3}){a}_{1}+({x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}){a}_{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $\\mathbf{b}$ can actually be written as a linear combination of only two vectors, ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the set $\\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2} \\}$ spans the subspace $U = \\textrm{Span} \\{ {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{a}_2}, {\\color{red}\\mathbf{a}_3} \\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4ff21f5d-5007-5b74-b135-bdb080fd5541" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3206 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "be88e95e-9faa-5bfe-b721-4eda45231dc0" :step/math-academy-id 17504 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120689}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120689 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119626 :question/id #uuid "51c289e1-f118-5444-bf1d-c7e8262c549f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4816"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050426}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399720}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"} :step/id #uuid "1169ef7f-a019-5cfa-8ef6-1d2ff514f2f1" :step/math-academy-id 17505 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120752}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120752 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121064 :tutorial/content "In the previous example, it was easy to see that one vector was a linear combination of the others. But what can we do when it's not so easy to tell? For example, consider the subspace\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find a subset of linearly independent vectors, we can put the vectors in a matrix and compute the reduced row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent (or redundant) vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\n**But watch out!** We need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one.\n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $U$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2c0f5ce4-8098-51b1-a25e-8cb4d84505c4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3945 :tutorial/title "Finding a Basis by Identifying Pivot Columns"} :step/id #uuid "dee9e822-2a94-59f6-9320-08d94fcf8912" :step/math-academy-id 23378 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119672 :question/id #uuid "f9601132-0450-51d2-8559-e83120f5fa2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6237"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f644bed-7adb-5907-87b4-99b1902e7850" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049723}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120399} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345009} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386585}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors by Identifying Pivot Columns"} :step/id #uuid "19b9d2e4-cc12-5198-97ee-5424e005fc32" :step/math-academy-id 23379 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121065 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we computed a basis of \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis that we found was\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}_3$ was not included in the basis. This means that $\\mathbf{a}_3$ can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\nNow, what if we want to find the exact linear combination of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ that yields $\\mathbf{a}_3?$ That is to say, we want to find $x_1, x_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis is equivalent to a system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we reduced this matrix to the *reduced* row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the third column of the *reduced* matrix tells us that $x_1=4$ and $x_2=5,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The matrix must be written in *reduced* row echelon form (with $1$'s for pivots and $0$'s above and below the pivots), not just row echelon form." :tutorial/id #uuid "14b13853-b5c6-5bc9-825c-a11cd19dff93" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3968 :tutorial/title "Finding a Particular Linear Combination of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "3fd42240-5623-532c-921e-a37910ca748c" :step/math-academy-id 23546 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119673 :question/id #uuid "5bdbcb40-c8d4-5191-bbcd-1b066e660f68" :question/math-academy-id "e-6257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049422} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050178}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386592} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393605}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"} :step/id #uuid "e1cd2039-adf1-5e16-9c01-414506e00c8e" :step/math-academy-id 23547}] :activity/title "Finding a Basis of a Span" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120049}]}
